  "null_model": {
    "method": "For each window, the observed network is compared with random networks that keep the same number of collaborators for every member. Each random network is made by repeatedly choosing two links and swapping their ends, so that the links move between the same members in a new pairing. Each replicate uses its own fixed seed, derived from one base seed, so the results repeat exactly. The score is the same as in the window table.",
    "reading": "z says how many standard deviations the observed value is from the average of the random networks. A large positive z means the network is more clustered, or more divided into groups, than its number of collaborators alone would produce. A z near 0 means the value is typical of chance. The two-sided p counts how often a random network lies at least as far from the average as the observed value, with a +1 correction. A z is not given when the random networks all give the same value.",
    "limits": "The null keeps each member's number of collaborators but ignores the work they share, so it is a test of structure given degree only. Profiles may be split or merged: two people may appear as one member, or one person as two. The sensitivity block shows how much the statistics move under a crude over-merge and under a random split. The network covers only the Atlas corpus, so the null describes that corpus, not the whole field.",
